<Guide
  title="What are Content Credentials (C2PA)? A plain-English guide | TagLens"
  description="Content Credentials (C2PA) are a signed history inside a photo or video saying who made it, with what tool, and whether AI was used. How they differ from IPTC fields and invisible watermarks."
  h1="What are Content Credentials (C2PA)?"
  updated="2026-11-21"
>
  <p><strong>Content Credentials</strong> are a tamper-evident history stored inside an image, video or audio file. They are built on an open standard called <strong>C2PA</strong> (Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity), backed by Adobe, Microsoft, Google, OpenAI, Meta, Sony, Leica, Samsung, the BBC and many others.</p>

  <h2>What they record</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Which tool</strong> created or edited the file (for example Adobe Photoshop, ChatGPT, a Leica camera).</li>
    <li><strong>What happened:</strong> created, opened, edited, cropped, placed, converted.</li>
    <li><strong>The source type:</strong> camera capture, AI-generated, AI-edited, screenshot and so on.</li>
    <li><strong>Ingredients:</strong> other files that went into it, each with its own history.</li>
    <li><strong>A digital signature</strong> from the tool’s maker, so you can tell if the file changed afterwards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three ways a file can say “AI”</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Content Credentials</th><th>IPTC field</th><th>Invisible watermark</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Where</td><td>A signed block in the file</td><td>A plain XMP text field</td><td>Hidden in the pixels</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Example</td><td>C2PA manifest from Firefly</td><td><code>DigitalSourceType</code></td><td>Google SynthID</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Survives a screenshot?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Usually yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Visible in TagLens?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Platforms combine all three, plus their own detection. That’s why a file can show nothing in a metadata tool and still be labelled.</p>

  <h2>The source types you’ll see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>digitalCapture</code>: taken with a camera.</li>
    <li><code>computationalCapture</code>: taken with a phone that processes the shot.</li>
    <li><code>trainedAlgorithmicMedia</code>: made by generative AI.</li>
    <li><code>compositeWithTrainedAlgorithmicMedia</code>: real image with an AI-generated part.</li>
    <li><code>algorithmicallyEnhanced</code>: improved by an algorithm, such as AI denoise or upscale.</li>
    <li><code>screenCapture</code>, <code>digitalArt</code>, <code>humanEdits</code> and others for non-AI sources.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why “does not verify” appears</h2>
  <p>The signature covers the file’s contents. If anything changes after signing (re-saving in an app that doesn’t support C2PA, or removing other metadata), the record is still there but no longer verifies. That tells you the file was altered after it was signed, not necessarily that someone did something wrong.</p>

  <h2>Check a file</h2>
  <p>Drop any image into <a href="/#checker">TagLens</a> to read its Content Credentials in your browser. You can also use the official <a href="https://contentcredentials.org/verify" rel="noopener">Content Credentials verify tool</a>, which uploads the file to check it.</p>
</Guide>
